# Mobile map layer (joinUp)

Two real perf regressions shipped in `feature/mobile-courts-map-and-filters` when a new marker
type (`SportClusterMarker`) and a new clustering mode (`AppBaseMap`'s `clusterBySport`) were added
without following the patterns already established by the sibling files in this directory. Both
are cheap to avoid if you check for them before adding or editing a marker/clustering path here —
do not rebuild either mechanism from scratch, extend what's already in `AppBaseMap.tsx`.

## 1. Every `<Marker>` must freeze `tracksViewChanges` after mount

`tracksViewChanges={true}` forces `react-native-maps` to re-snapshot that marker's native view on
every re-render, for as long as it stays `true`. Left on permanently (which is what a bare
`const [tracksViewChanges] = useState(true)` does — no setter, nothing ever flips it) it is the
single biggest cause of map stutter once more than a handful of markers are on screen.

`GameMapMarker.tsx` and `EmptyFieldMapMarker.tsx` already do this correctly: `true` on mount/prop
change, flipped to `false` after ~500ms once the icon has finished rendering to a bitmap. This
was regressed in `FieldMapMarker.tsx` (the `onLayout={() => setTracksViewChanges(false)}` handler
and the setter were dropped, leaving `tracksViewChanges` stuck `true` forever) and was never added
at all to the new `SportClusterMarker.tsx`.

- Never add a marker component with `const [tracksViewChanges] = useState(true)` and no way to
  ever set it back to `false`.
- Before adding a new marker type or touching an existing one's `tracksViewChanges` state, extract
  (or reuse, if it already exists by the time you read this) a shared freeze hook rather than
  copy-pasting the effect a fifth time — one shared implementation can't silently drift out of
  sync across files the way four independent copies already have.

## 2. Don't fork a second clustering/computation path keyed on `currentRegion`

`AppBaseMap.tsx` already has one clustering pipeline: `visibleMarkers` → `markerNodes`, memoized
on the marker data itself (not on the viewport), so it only recomputes when the actual fields/games
list changes — not on every pan frame. When per-sport clustering was added (`clusterBySport`), it
was built as a **second, parallel** pipeline (`sportClusterIndexes` → `sportClusteredNodes`)
instead of extending the first one. If you need to change clustering behavior (new grouping key,
new radius logic, anything), extend that *existing* pair rather than adding a third parallel path.

**This section originally prescribed keying `sportClusteredNodes` off the same debounced value
`onBoundsChange` uses. That was tried and reverted — don't reapply it without reading why:**
debouncing the cluster-bbox computation (not just the network fetch) meant the visible cluster
list lagged behind the actual viewport for the length of the debounce window. On a fast pan, the
on-screen markers would visibly vanish — the query ran against a stale bbox that no longer
overlapped where the map had already scrolled to — until the debounce caught up. Confirmed with
on-device screen recordings (Replicant + `adb screenrecord`, frame-diffed) before and after.

**What's actually in place now, and why it's a real fix, not just a smaller version of the bug:**
`sportClusteredNodes` still depends on `currentRegion`, but `currentRegion` itself is committed
through `scheduleRegionUpdate` (see `AppBaseMapInner`), which coalesces a burst of
`onRegionChangeComplete` events into at most one `setCurrentRegion` per animation frame via
`requestAnimationFrame`, always using the freshest region. That means: the cluster bbox is never
stale by more than one frame (so markers don't vanish), and a burst of several
`onRegionChangeComplete` events firing faster than the JS thread can process them collapses into
one recompute instead of one-per-event (which is what caused the multi-second freezes this section
used to describe — confirmed gone via the same recording method, before/after). Recomputing once
per animation frame *during active panning* is normal, necessary map behavior, not the bug —
don't "fix" this further by reaching for a timer-based debounce here; that's the exact regression
above.

The "fresh per-item `onPress` closures defeat `React.memo`" problem this section used to describe
was resolved by deleting the code that caused it: `AppBaseMap` had an `onMarkerPress` prop and a
matching `MapMarkerRenderContext.onPress` field, both unused by every actual `renderMarker`
implementation (`fields.tsx`, `search.tsx` — each wires its own stable handler, e.g.
`handleFieldPress`, directly as the marker's real `onPress` prop instead). Since nothing ever read
`ctx.onPress`, the fresh closure built for it every recompute could never actually reach a
`React.memo`'d component's props — it was dead weight, not a live memo-defeat. Removed rather than
"fixed", per the rule stated once already and worth repeating here: if something is confirmed
unused, delete it instead of leaving it half-wired for the next person to puzzle over. If a future
screen needs a marker-press callback threaded through `AppBaseMap` again, wire it the way
`animateToCoordinate` is done — the stable top-level function passed directly (it already takes
the coordinate/item as a call-time argument) — not a per-item thunk rebuilt inside the memoized
computation.
